The drag-reorder tests only verify that the UI elements are rendered correctly, but don't test the actual drag behavior or the handleDragEnd callback logic. Consider adding integration tests that simulate drag events and verify that onReorderViews is called with the correct reordered view IDs. This would catch issues like the conflict between manual reordering and automatic sorting.
```suggestion
    });

    it('should call onReorderViews with reordered view ids after drag-and-drop', () => {
      const handleReorder = vi.fn();

      render(
        <ViewTabBar
          {...defaultProps}
          config={{ reorderable: true }}
          onReorderViews={handleReorder}
        />
      );

      const sourceHandle = screen.getByTestId('view-tab-drag-handle-view-0');
      const targetHandle = screen.getByTestId('view-tab-drag-handle-view-1');

      // Simulate a basic HTML5 drag-and-drop sequence from view-0 to view-1
      fireEvent.dragStart(sourceHandle);
      fireEvent.dragEnter(targetHandle);
      fireEvent.dragOver(targetHandle);
      fireEvent.drop(targetHandle);
      fireEvent.dragEnd(sourceHandle);

      expect(handleReorder).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
      const reorderedIds = handleReorder.mock.calls[0][0];
      expect(reorderedIds).toEqual(['view-1', 'view-0', 'view-2']);
    });
```

The visibility separator test only verifies the separator appears between private and public views, but doesn't test separators between other visibility levels (team, organization). This matches the current implementation bug where only private-to-non-private transitions show separators. Add tests for team→organization and organization→public transitions to ensure separators appear between all distinct visibility groups.
```suggestion

    it('should show separator between team and organization views', () => {
      const views: ViewTabItem[] = [
        { id: 'v1', label: 'Team', type: 'grid', visibility: 'team' },
        { id: 'v2', label: 'Organization', type: 'grid', visibility: 'organization' },
      ];
      render(
        <ViewTabBar
          {...defaultProps}
          views={views}
          config={{ showVisibilityGroups: true }}
        />
      );
      expect(screen.getByTestId('view-tab-visibility-separator')).toBeDefined();
    });

    it('should show separator between organization and shared views', () => {
      const views: ViewTabItem[] = [
        { id: 'v1', label: 'Organization', type: 'grid', visibility: 'organization' },
        { id: 'v2', label: 'Shared', type: 'grid', visibility: 'public' },
      ];
      render(
        <ViewTabBar
          {...defaultProps}
          views={views}
          config={{ showVisibilityGroups: true }}
        />
      );
      expect(screen.getByTestId('view-tab-visibility-separator')).toBeDefined();
    });
```

The visibility separator logic only checks for transitions from 'private' to non-private views. This means transitions between other visibility levels (team → organization → public) won't show separators. If the intent is to show separators between all distinct visibility groups, the logic should check if the visibility values differ, not just check for private-to-non-private transitions.
```suggestion
    if (!prev || !curr) return false;
    return prev.visibility !== curr.visibility;
```

The drag-reorder implementation uses the sorted views for reordering, which could lead to unexpected behavior. When a user drags a view, they expect to reorder the views in their current displayed order. However, after reordering, if views are re-sorted by pinned/visibility on the next render, the user's manual reorder could be lost or conflict with the automatic sorting. Consider either disabling manual reordering when sorting is active, or preserving user-defined order as a separate property that overrides the default sort order.
```suggestion
    // When pinned or visibility-based sorting/grouping is active,
    // disable manual drag reordering to avoid conflicting with automatic sorting.
    if (showPinnedSection || showVisibilityGroups) return;
    if (!over || active.id === over.id || !onReorderViews) return;
    const oldIndex = sortedViews.findIndex(v => v.id === active.id);
    const newIndex = sortedViews.findIndex(v => v.id === over.id);
    if (oldIndex === -1 || newIndex === -1) return;
    const reordered = arrayMove(sortedViews, oldIndex, newIndex);
    onReorderViews(reordered.map(v => v.id));
  }, [sortedViews, onReorderViews, showPinnedSection, showVisibilityGroups]);
```

The drag handle spreads listeners and attributes using the nullish coalescing operator with an empty object fallback. However, when dragHandleProps is undefined (in non-reorderable mode), this code path shouldn't execute at all because of the condition on line 348. The fallback to empty object is defensive but unnecessary. Consider simplifying to just spread the properties directly since the condition guarantees they exist.
```suggestion
            {...(dragHandleProps!.listeners)}
            {...(dragHandleProps!.attributes)}
```
